{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 2700 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "bf150acd76dd307fc1915e48e4a872303fe30a1f4ea450bb04f3857fe8738ca0" :edb/db-before-t 2699 :edb/db-after-t 2700 :edb/tx-data [[13194139536012 50 #inst "2026-10-09T12:54:08.076Z" 13194139536012 true] [17592186379305 1100 "Given that $L{{e}^{at}}=\\frac{1}{s-a}$ for $s>a$, find the Laplace transform of\n$g(t)={\\int }_{0}^{t}{e}^{-3τ}\\,\\text{d}τ$\nfor $s>-3.$" 13194139536012 false] [17592186379305 1100 "Given that $L{{e}^{at}}=\\frac{1}{s-a}$ for $s>a$, find the Laplace transform of\n$g(t)={\\int }_{0}^{t}{e}^{-3τ}\\,\\text{d}τ$\nfor $s>0.$" 13194139536012 true] [17592186379305 1145 "Evaluate the integral first:\n\n$$g(t)=\\int_0^t e^{-3\\tau}\\,d\\tau=\\frac{1-e^{-3t}}3.$$\n\nFor $s>0$, both exponential integrals converge, and\n\n$$\\mathcal L\\{g\\}(s)=\\frac13\\left(\\frac1s-\\frac1{s+3}\\right)=\\frac1{s(s+3)}.$$\n\nThis is option a. The integrand $g(t)$ tends to $1/3$. In fact, for $t\\ge1$, $g(t)\\ge(1-e^{-3})/3>0$, so the defining Laplace integral diverges for every real $s\\le0$. Thus the maximal real convergence domain is $s>0$. The original source's $s>-3$ is the convergence condition for $e^{-3t}$, not for its integral; extending the rational expression to negative $s$ does not extend the transform." 13194139536012 true] [17592186379305 1145 "We know that if $L{f(t)}=F(s)$, then\n\n$$\nL{{\\int }_{0}^{t}f(τ)\\,\\text{d}τ}=\\frac{F(s)}{s}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(t)={e}^{-3t}.$ Thus, for $s>-3,$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & =L{f(t)} \\\\  & =L{{e}^{-3t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s+3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the integral identity above, we have that for $s>-3,$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{g(t)} & =L{{\\int }_{0}^{t}{e}^{-3τ}\\,\\text{d}τ} \\\\  & =\\frac{F(s)}{s} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s(s+3)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536012 false]] :edb/tempids {}}
